  "worked_solution": "Let $P$ be the matrix that represents the rotation and let $Q$ be the matrix that represents the shear. These are given by the following elementary transformation matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) & -\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) & \\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Q & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the combined transformation can be represented by the matrix $QP:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}QP & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
